A||N||3||PocketRocket||New Clutch...Engine Removal?||||1||
Z||000000||PocketRocket||09-17-2000||06:37 PM||||Will the engine have to be removed beforeI can change teh clutch?<P>------------------<BR>-Larry B<BR>'86 SE 2.8L||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||09-17-2000||11:02 PM||||Speaking from nonexperience    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">, that is, reading posts in this forum, it <B>is</B> possible to do a clutch job on V6 Fieros without removing the engine.  In essence, the methods are as follows:<P>Method 1) Remove all cradle bolts except the RF one.  This allows "rocking the cradle" (I love that expression!) downward, to allow the transaxle to slide out.  See: <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#trany" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#trany</A> <P>Method 2) Someone (fairly recently) posted that they were able to support the engine, then slide the transaxle outboard (left) until it contacted the frame.  Apparently, this allowed about 4" of space to reach in and remove the pressure plate.  Last I knew, this person had removed the old clutch, and was going to install the new one. See: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005658.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005658.html</A> <P>Try doing a search - you'll undoubtedly learn some useful things. <P>I have read many times that it really isn't too painful to drop "cradle and all," then separate the trans. from engine while it's all out of the car.   I plan to do it this way on mine - that way I can do some things to the engine (like inspect and clean, and fix some broken exhaust manifold bolts) while it's out of there.   <P>Hope this helps    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 09-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||09-18-2000||03:52 PM||||I just added links to the above post.  Hope they help you (and others). <P>In case it makes you feel better, I have a squeaky clutch pedal in one car.  Probably just needs a bushing, but I'm almost afraid to touch it, lest I find an hydraulic leak or something else   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> !<P>Another Fiero has had a slipping clutch since the day I bought it (used).  It's the Isuzu 5-speed transaxle, and not a daily-use car, so I'm hesitant to mess with it, too.<P>My Fiero GT's clutch works perfectly, so  naturally, the "cradle and all" will likely come out, so that I can replace exhaust manifold bolts, port the manifolds, and install the recall package (I feel better doing it myself than trust dealership).<P>When I get enough time+ ambition to do these chores, I may have experience with all three methods  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> (?)<BR>    <BR> ||||reg||2
Z||000003||EdsB52||09-19-2000||03:11 AM||||I've pulled many engines from Fieros with a special bar I drew up and had made. But the last time I did a clutch in a 2.5, I tried to support the engine and drop the craddle. It sucked. I broke my 02 sensor and it took at least as long as pulling the whole deal, and added much frustration. I think I even bent the exhaust a little.||||reg||1
